    "faq_peri": {
      "type": "faq",
      "blocks": {
        "q_heat": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "How hot is this, really?",
            "text": "<p>Hotter than a chili flake and milder than most hot sauces, but it depends entirely on the blend you buy &mdash; bird&rsquo;s eye chilies vary wildly and commercial blends are cut with paprika at very different ratios. Taste a pinch on a fingertip before you commit the fish. If it is too much, cut the blend with smoked paprika one to one; the colour and the smoke stay, the heat halves.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_sauce": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Can I use peri-peri sauce instead of the dry blend?",
            "text": "<p>Yes, with two adjustments. Use about half as much, because the sauce is already carrying oil and acid, and pat the fillets before they go on the heat &mdash; a wet surface steams instead of charring. Keep the rest of the bottle for the table, which is where it does its best work anyway.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_stick": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "My fish stuck to the grill. What went wrong?",
            "text": "<p>Almost certainly a cold surface. The sheet pan needs to preheat on the grate with the grill, not go on at the last second &mdash; fish laid on cool metal grabs it before a crust can form. The other cause is moving it too early: leave it for the full three to four minutes and it releases on its own.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_longer": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Can I marinate it longer than ten minutes?",
            "text": "<p>Not usefully. Peri-peri carries vinegar and lemon, and past about twenty minutes the acid starts to firm and opaque the outside of the fillet the way a ceviche would. You lose the contrast between charred crust and soft centre, which is the point of the dish. Ten minutes is not a shortcut; it is the recipe.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_nogrill": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "I do not have a grill.",
            "text": "<p>A cast-iron pan over medium-high does this well indoors &mdash; same timing, same instruction to leave it alone. A sheet pan under a high broiler about six inches from the element gets closest to the char, and needs no flipping if you are cooking skin-side down. Run the extractor either way.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_thaw": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "How should I thaw Seatopia seafood?",
            "text": "<p>Overnight in the refrigerator in the sealed pack is best, about 12 hours. In a hurry, submerge the sealed pack in a bowl of chilled water for 20 to 30 minutes, changing the water once. Never thaw at room temperature, and never in warm water.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "q_heat",
        "q_sauce",
        "q_stick",
        "q_longer",
        "q_nogrill",
        "q_thaw"
      ],
